option.shadowDistance.comment=How far away terrain can cast or receive \
	real-time shadows; past that distance it falls back to approximate static \
	shadows based on skylight. Lower improves frame rate, and the effective \
	distance is capped by View Distance in Video Settings.

option.SURFACE_COLORS=Surface colors
value.SURFACE_COLORS.STANDARD=Standard
value.SURFACE_COLORS.NONE=None (whiteworld)
value.SURFACE_COLORS.VERTEX_COLOR=Vertex color (foliage)
option.SURFACE_COLORS.comment=Draws surfaces without their own colour: None \
	treats everything as white, Vertex colour drops only the texture colour so \
	foliage stays green. For debugging lighting.

option.PBR_FORMAT=Material format
value.PBR_FORMAT.PBR_OFF=Off
value.PBR_FORMAT.LAB_PBR=LabPBR
option.PBR_FORMAT.comment=Reads the normal (_n) and specular (_s) maps of a \
	PBR resource pack (shaderLABS LabPBR 1.3); each material feature below can \
	still be switched off separately, and only the block atlas is affected. Turn \
	it Off to skip the reads without a PBR pack - packs with no material maps \
	decode to a flat normal, no reflection and no emission.

option.PBR_TANGENT_ATTRIBUTE=Geometry tangents
option.PBR_TANGENT_ATTRIBUTE.comment=Builds the material tangent frame from \
	the tangent the geometry actually carries, instead of rebuilding one from \
	screen-space derivatives. Without it the normal map can look inverted past a \
	certain distance, which no mip setting fixes. On by default as a \
	correctness fix; geometry with no tangent falls back to the old method \
	automatically.

option.PBR_NORMAL_MAP=Normal mapping
option.PBR_NORMAL_MAP.comment=Makes the normal map bend the surface normal, so \
	texture detail reacts to the light's direction. Off, the height channel of \
	the same texture is simply not read.

option.PBR_NORMAL_STRENGTH=Normal strength
option.PBR_NORMAL_STRENGTH.comment=How far the normal map is allowed to bend \
	the surface normal. Raise it if surface detail is too subtle, lower it if the \
	lighting looks noisy or dirty.

option.PBR_MATERIAL_MAX_LOD=Material detail limit
option.PBR_MATERIAL_MAX_LOD.comment=The finest mip level the block atlas's \
	material maps may be sampled at; 0 samples full resolution everywhere. Past a \
	sprite's edge the atlas is black, so past a certain level the normal map \
	tilts or inverts - a hard line across the ground at a fixed distance. Raise \
	it to restore filtering on distant material maps at the risk of that artefact \
	returning; Normal fade distance keeps it away.

option.PBR_NORMAL_FADE_LOD=Normal fade distance
option.PBR_NORMAL_FADE_LOD.comment=The mip level at which normal mapping has \
	fully faded to a flat surface. A normal map finer than a screen pixel only \
	shimmers, so this removes that noise; lower it if distant terrain looks \
	noisy, raise it to keep the detail and accept some sparkle.

option.PBR_PARALLAX=Parallax occlusion
option.PBR_PARALLAX.comment=Moves what the material maps and the block texture \
	are read at along the direction you are looking from, by the height the pack \
	stores in the alpha channel of its normal map (LabPBR's height, where 1.0 is \
	"not displaced"). The surface then shows real depth: the near side of a \
	groove hides what is behind it, a mortar line shows its own edge, and the \
	colour, normal and specular come from the texel you are actually looking at \
	rather than from the one the flat face is drawn at. Off by default, because \
	it is the one material feature that costs real time - a loop of texture \
	reads per pixel of every block - and because a pack with no height channel \
	has nothing to displace.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_STEPS=Parallax steps
option.PBR_PARALLAX_STEPS.comment=How many steps the ray takes across the \
	height field before the crossing it finds is refined. Each step reads one \
	height, and the first step that lands past the surface ends the march, so \
	more steps find shallower grooves that fewer walk straight past - which \
	reads as a groove that appears from one side and vanishes from another. It \
	is also the width of the displaced surface's staircase: the crossing can \
	only be found between two samples, so the surface is a set of steps one \
	spacing apart, and at a grazing angle a spacing covers several pixels and \
	reads as plates stacked up the surface. One budget pays for both answers: \
	the march starts at a per-pixel offset inside its first spacing so that the \
	staircase is noise rather than bands, and its samples are crowded towards \
	the shallow end of the depth, where a height map's crossings actually are - \
	so the same step count resolves roughly twice as much of what you can see. \
	The cost is one texture read per step for every covered pixel; halve it to \
	feel what it buys, and expect the deepest parts of a height map to go \
	missing.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_REFINE=Parallax refine
option.PBR_PARALLAX_REFINE.comment=How many times the crossing found above is \
	halved again by a binary search between the last step above the surface and \
	the first below it. The march stops at the first sample past the crossing, \
	and it also starts at a per-pixel offset inside its first step, so what it \
	finds is up to one step out in a way that differs from pixel to pixel: with \
	this low, what is left of that is a fine noise over the displaced surface, \
	and each halving here takes it down by half for one texture read. That is \
	why it can look as though it does nothing at the bottom of its range - the \
	staircase itself belongs to Parallax steps.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_SMOOTH=Smooth parallax
option.PBR_PARALLAX_SMOOTH.comment=Whether the height the march reads is \
	interpolated between the texels of the height map, or taken from the texel \
	the coordinate lands in. The interpolation is done by the shader itself, four \
	texture reads per step, because the material atlas is built with \
	nearest-neighbour sampling and a filtered read against it returns one texel \
	whatever it is asked for. On is smoother: the height between two texels is a \
	ramp, so the displaced surface has no edge at a texel boundary and moves \
	continuously as you do - at the price of a ridge along any border whose two \
	sides store very different heights. Off reads the texel itself, which is the \
	height map as the pack authored it - one flat plate per texel, and a \
	staircase by construction - and it is where the sparkle along the edges of a \
	deep groove comes from. Off, the default, for two reasons: it is a quarter of \
	the texture reads, and at the step count this ships with the plates are no \
	longer the widest thing on the surface. Turn it on when a groove's edge \
	sparkles and the terraces are what you notice.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_DEPTH=Parallax depth
option.PBR_PARALLAX_DEPTH.comment=How far the height field reaches below the \
	face, as a fraction of the sprite, at a texel whose height is 0. This is the \
	one number that has to be guessed: a height map says how the shape varies \
	but never how tall it is, and LabPBR's own answer is 25% of the texture, so \
	0.25 in the list is the scale the format asks for and the default is a little \
	shallower. A sixteenth of a sprite is a mortar line; half of one is a groove \
	so deep that its sides are longer than the surface they are cut into, and the \
	top of the list is a whole block, which is a hole rather than a carving - \
	raise it until the wall reads as carved, and stop before it reads as a sheet \
	of paper with holes in it. A pack that uses only the top tenth of the height \
	range displaces a tenth of this. The sprite is the face's own texture, so on \
	a full face this is a depth in blocks and does not change with the resource \
	pack's resolution; a program drawn from a texture that is not a sprite of an \
	atlas has no such scale and gets no displacement at all.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_MAX_OFFSET=Parallax depth ceiling
option.PBR_PARALLAX_MAX_OFFSET.comment=The most the displacement is ever allowed \
	to lean, as a fraction of the sprite. It works with Parallax depth rather than \
	instead of it, and the march uses whichever of the two is lower, so at the \
	defaults it does nothing - set Parallax depth to 1.0 and this to 0.1 to see \
	what it does. Its top end is past a whole block, which is deeper than any \
	height map should be read as; it is there so that this is never what stops a \
	pack from being read the way it was authored. Note what it is not: it is not a \
	limit on how far the ray travels across the surface, which would be a limit on \
	the view angle and would flatten the relief the more nearly parallel to the \
	surface you looked - that is what this option used to do, and what it no \
	longer does.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_DISTANCE=Parallax distance
option.PBR_PARALLAX_DISTANCE.comment=How far away the displacement is applied, \
	in metres, fading out over the last half of it. Past a certain distance a \
	pixel covers more than one texel of the height map, so what the march \
	computes stops meaning anything and what is left is the cost and the noise \
	of a coordinate that wanders inside a texel. Sixteen blocks is where a 16 \
	pixel pack stops showing the effect at a normal field of view; raise it for a \
	high resolution pack, lower it to 8 to see how much of the frame the march \
	was costing.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW=Parallax self-shadow
option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW.comment=Whether a texel standing above the ones \
	behind it, seen from the sun, darkens them. A displaced surface is a change \
	of shape, and a shape lit from one side needs to cast its own shadow or it \
	reads as painted on - this is the one thing in the pack that darkens direct \
	sunlight for a reason the shadow map cannot see, because the shadow map \
	knows about blocks and this is a shape inside one. On by default, because that \
	groove side is the part of the displacement no other channel can say. It is \
	also the most expensive thing here - half of the displacement's cost again, on \
	the fragments the displacement covers - so if the frame rate matters more than \
	the shape, this is the switch to reach for rather than a lower step count: \
	turning it off leaves the displacement itself exactly as it was.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW_STRENGTH=Self-shadow strength
option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW_STRENGTH.comment=How much of the direct light a \
	self-shadow is allowed to take away; 1.0 is as much as the geometry allows. \
	Lower it if the grooves look painted in rather than cut in - below about a \
	quarter the shadow is hiding the displacement more than it explains it. No \
	effect while Parallax self-shadow is off.


option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO=Material occlusion
option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO.comment=Darkens the ambient and block light reaching a \
	fragment by the occlusion baked into the blue channel of the normal map, \
	LabPBR's third value there alongside the normal in X and Y and the height in \
	alpha. Off by default, as it is the one material feature that changes how \
	bright familiar blocks are; only ambient and block light are affected, so \
	sunlight is never darkened twice.

option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO_STRENGTH=Occlusion strength
option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO_STRENGTH.comment=How much of that occlusion is applied; \
	1.0 uses the channel exactly as the pack authored it, so lower it if the pack \
	overdoes it. No effect with Material occlusion off.

option.PBR_SPECULAR=Specular reflections
option.PBR_SPECULAR.comment=Whether the smoothness and reflectance (F0) in the \
	specular map drive a specular highlight: rough surfaces get a broad, soft \
	sheen, smooth ones a sharp highlight, which is the difference between \
	polished stone and plastic.

option.PBR_SPECULAR_STRENGTH=Specular strength
option.PBR_SPECULAR_STRENGTH.comment=How strong the specular reflection is. \
	1.0 is physically correct, but a real dielectric reflects only about 4% of \
	the light hitting it, so it is far fainter than most people expect; raise it \
	if surfaces still look flat, lower it towards 1.0 if everything looks wet or \
	plasticky.

option.PBR_LIGHT_SIZE=Sun and moon size
option.PBR_LIGHT_SIZE.comment=How big the sun and the moon are as far as the \
	highlight they make on a surface is concerned. Both are treated as points, \
	which gives a highlight a pixel wide instead of the disc a mirror really \
	shows; 1.0 is the sun's real angular size of about half a degree, 0.0 being \
	the point light this used to be. Larger values exaggerate the disc for the \
	look of it; the sun and moon drawn in the sky do not change.

option.PBR_ENERGY_CONSERVATION=Energy conservation
option.PBR_ENERGY_CONSERVATION.comment=Weights the diffuse light by the part \
	of it the surface did not reflect, so diffuse and specular cannot add up to \
	more light than arrived. Without it a shiny surface can be brighter than the \
	light falling on it, while a metal that should have almost no diffuse \
	response still takes half of one and reads as shiny plastic. Off by default, \
	as it changes everything with a specular map at once; pair it with a Specular \
	strength of 1.0. Bright metals go noticeably darker in caves and at night - \
	worth trying rather than assuming.

option.PBR_REFLECTIONS=Sky reflections
option.PBR_REFLECTIONS.comment=Whether a material reflects the sky, with a \
	direction and a roughness, instead of a flat wash of the light around it. \
	With it on, the color comes from the sky model along the reflected ray, and a \
	rough surface has that ray bent towards its own normal; it reflects the sky \
	and not the world, and one sky model evaluation per covered pixel makes it \
	the most expensive option here.

option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_STRENGTH=Sky reflection strength
option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_STRENGTH.comment=How strong the sky reflection is; 1.0 \
	is the value it was tuned at. Has no effect while Sky reflections is off, nor \
	indoors or underground, where there is no sky to reflect.

option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_ROUGHNESS=Roughness spread
option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_ROUGHNESS.comment=How far a rough surface bends the \
	reflected ray towards its own normal: at 1.0 it looks straight up and shows \
	the average sky above it, at 0.0 roughness does not affect the direction and \
	it shows the mirror image a polished surface does. No effect while Sky \
	reflections is off.

option.PBR_REFLECTION_SKY_MIN=Sky needed to reflect
option.PBR_REFLECTION_SKY_MIN.comment=How much sky a surface has to see before \
	it reflects any of it. Minecraft's sky light spreads sideways under an \
	overhang and passes through glass, so a block can be lit as if outdoors while \
	nothing of the sky is visible from it - keeping this high stops a polished \
	block in a shallow cave reflecting a noon sky. A surface lit through a glass \
	window is indistinguishable from one in the open. Only the sky part of the \
	reflection is affected.

option.PBR_REFLECTION_SMOOTHNESS_MIN=Smoothness needed to reflect
option.PBR_REFLECTION_SMOOTHNESS_MIN.comment=How smooth a surface has to be \
	before it reflects the sky at all; below this it reflects nothing. At the \
	default, anything rougher than about 0.25 in this pack's roughness reflects \
	nothing. Raise it to restrict reflections to shinier blocks, lower it to let \
	them onto duller ones; 0.0 disables the gate, and it also scales the traced \
	reflection.

option.PBR_METAL_REFLECTION_STRENGTH=Metal reflection strength
option.PBR_METAL_REFLECTION_STRENGTH.comment=How strong a metal's environment \
	reflection is, separately from the strength everything else uses, which is \
	not about metals at all. 1.0 matches the reference packs; turn it down if \
	metals are too bright in a scene.

option.PBR_REFLECTION_BLUR=Reflection blur
option.PBR_REFLECTION_BLUR.comment=How far the reflection of the world is \
	spread out at a roughness of 1.0, in pixels. A rough surface should not show \
	the sharp mirror image a smooth one does: a whole cone of directions arrives \
	at the same pixel, and spreading the sample over the cone is what makes \
	brushed or frosted metal read that way. Only surfaces past the reflection \
	gate pay, eight samples instead of one.

option.PBR_SSR=Screen-space reflections
option.PBR_SSR.comment=Whether a reflection may include the world and not only \
	the sky, by tracing the reflected ray through the depth buffer. Sky \
	reflections alone give everything with a reflectance the same wash of sky; \
	with this on, a polished block reflects the terrain, the trees and whatever \
	else the ray finds on screen. It can only reflect what is on screen and only \
	from the previous frame, and a ray that finds nothing falls back to the sky. \
	Very expensive, so turn it off if the frame budget is tight.

option.PBR_SSR_STEPS=Reflection trace steps
option.PBR_SSR_STEPS.comment=How many steps the reflection trace may take; \
	only the material reflections use this. More steps find more hits and refine \
	them better, and the cost is paid whether or not anything is found - the \
	first setting to lower if reflections are too expensive.


option.PBR_DEFAULT_MATERIAL=Default material
option.PBR_DEFAULT_MATERIAL.comment=Whether blocks the pack gives no usable \
	specular data for fall back to a plain dielectric; without it they decode to \
	no reflectance and fully rough. Turn it off to see exactly what the pack \
	authored.

option.PBR_DEFAULT_ROUGHNESS=Default roughness
option.PBR_DEFAULT_ROUGHNESS.comment=The roughness given to those fallback \
	materials; lower is glossier. It is also how a block looks with no specular \
	data at all, so it sets an ordinary block's baseline shine.

option.PBR_METALS=Hardcoded metals
option.PBR_METALS.comment=Whether to use the reflectance values LabPBR \
	hardcodes for iron, gold, aluminum, chrome, copper, lead, platinum and \
	silver. Off, they are treated as albedo-based metals instead, far closer than \
	treating them as ordinary dielectrics.

option.PBR_METAL_DIFFUSE=Metal diffuse
option.PBR_METAL_DIFFUSE.comment=How much of its diffuse response a metal \
	loses. A metal has none in reality: light either reflects off it or is \
	absorbed. 0.0 keeps all of the diffuse response and looks like coloured \
	plastic; 1.0 removes it entirely and is physically correct. Lower it if \
	metals come out too dark in the dark.

option.PBR_SUBSURFACE=Subsurface scattering
option.PBR_SUBSURFACE.comment=Adds the light that scatters through a thin \
	surface, using the amount stored in the blue channel of the specular map: \
	LabPBR values up to 64 of 255 are porosity, and anything above is how much \
	light the material lets through. What this adds is the glow of a backlit \
	leaf, whose brightest side faces away from the sun, and a material storing \
	nothing in that channel is unaffected.

option.PBR_SSS_STRENGTH=Scattering strength
option.PBR_SSS_STRENGTH.comment=How much scattered light is added, 0.7 being \
	the tuned value. Raise it for a stronger glow through leaves, lower it if \
	foliage looks lit from the inside.

option.PBR_POROSITY_WETNESS=Wet porous surfaces
option.PBR_POROSITY_WETNESS.comment=Whether surfaces the pack marked as porous \
	get darker while wet, which is what makes brick, dirt and wood look soaked \
	after rain rather than merely shiny. LabPBR stores porosity in the lower half \
	of the specular map's blue channel, and the effect is scaled by sky light, so \
	it only shows outdoors in rain, on materials a pack marked porous.

option.PBR_TRANSLUCENT=PBR on ice and glass
option.PBR_TRANSLUCENT.comment=Whether ice and other translucent solids take \
	their reflectance and their normal from the resource pack. Ice is drawn by \
	the translucent pass, which gives water 0.1 reflectance and everything else \
	none, so it reflects nothing except at grazing angles whatever the pack \
	authored; with this on ice is decoded like any other block. Water is \
	deliberately excluded, its normal coming from Steadfast's own wave model.

option.PBR_HAND_ITEM_MATERIALS=PBR on held items (not blocks)
option.PBR_HAND_ITEM_MATERIALS.comment=Whether a held item - not a held block \
	- reads the resource pack's PBR materials as well. On, it is what makes a \
	blade catch the sun or a polished tool mirror the sky. A pack that ships no \
	LabPBR maps for its items leaves the item atlas coordinates reading another \
	sprite's material, so turn this off for such a pack. A held item that looks \
	see-through is not this option's doing - that was the hand's environment \
	reflection, which no longer happens.

option.PBR_HAND_ITEMS=PBR on held items
option.PBR_HAND_ITEMS.comment=Whether the item or block in your hand gets PBR \
	materials as well. A held block is drawn from the block atlas and so carries \
	the same material data as the same block placed in the world; a held item \
	that is not a block reads its material from the item atlas instead.

option.PBR_ENTITIES=PBR on entities
option.PBR_ENTITIES.comment=Whether mobs and other entities get PBR materials. \
	This one is an experiment: the block atlas is the only atlas Iris and \
	OptiFine are documented to build material maps for, so an entity may end up \
	shaded with some arbitrary block's materials. To check, turn it on, look at a \
	mob and select the Occlusion channel under the material debug view - a flat, \
	uniform picture means no material data is bound and this should go back off.

option.PBR_DEBUG=Material debug view [build 506]
option.DEFERRED_DEBUG=Deferred split view (geometry vs post)
option.DEFERRED_DEBUG.comment=Shows what the geometry wrote on the left half \
	of the screen and what this pass writes back on the right. Only the \
	right-hand half says anything: it means the geometry does not have it and \
	this pass made it. On both halves is the ordinary case and proves nothing. \
	Use one split view at a time.
option.TAA_DEBUG=Temporal anti-aliasing split view
option.TAA_DEBUG.comment=Shows the picture this pass was handed on the left \
	half of the screen and the one it produced on the right. Watch which half a \
	blot appears in first, not how far it spreads: the two halves are the same \
	frame and the history buffer is shared, so its extent always crosses the \
	middle and says nothing. Left means the source is upstream, right means the \
	history makes it. Does nothing with temporal anti-aliasing off.
option.SSS_GAIN=Sky shadow depth gain
option.AMBIENT_OCCLUSION=Ambient occlusion (experimental)
option.AMBIENT_OCCLUSION.comment=Darkens corners, wall bases and block joins: \
	this pack, like the game, lights a surface by its light level alone. Being \
	screen-space, it misses thin things such as grass and fences. Off by default: \
	it changes the brightness of the whole picture, and it runs before the \
	temporal resolve, so anti-aliasing averages its noise away. If turning it on \
	seems to do nothing, turn on the occlusion debug view below; Voxy terrain has \
	none of it yet.
option.AO_STRENGTH=Occlusion strength
option.AO_STRENGTH.comment=How much of it to apply. The default 1.0 is the \
	full amount and the ceiling - above it a fully occluded pixel goes negative, \
	drawn as black. Setting it to 0 turns the effect off without touching the \
	option above.
option.AO_RADIUS=Occlusion radius
option.AO_RADIUS.comment=How far a sample may be from the point it tests, in \
	blocks: about a block reaches the corner where two walls meet and the gap \
	under a fence. Raising it costs nothing extra, but the same samples over a \
	larger disk come out coarser as well as broader.
option.AO_SAMPLES=Occlusion samples
option.AO_SAMPLES.comment=How many samples are taken per pixel: the pass's \
	cost, one depth fetch each, and the noise dial - doubling the count is about \
	a third less noise for twice the work. Default 24; raise it if the result \
	flickers, lower it if it costs too much.
option.AO_DEBUG=Occlusion debug view
option.AO_DEBUG.comment=Shows the occlusion measurement itself: white where \
	much of the pixel's surroundings is unblocked, black where all of it is \
	blocked. It separates "the occlusion is not running" from "it is computing \
	zero". Dark at corners and joins is correct; a uniformly grey screen means \
	the tolerance is wrong.
screen.END=The End
screen.END_BODY_SETTINGS=The End's body
screen.SKY_SETTINGS=Sky, sun and moon
screen.STAR_SETTINGS=Stars
screen.CLOUDS=Clouds
screen.CLOUD_BASIC_SETTINGS=Cloud basics
screen.CLOUD_VOLUMETRIC_SETTINGS=Volumetric clouds
screen.CLOUD_SCATTER_SETTINGS=Multiple scattering
screen.WATER_STYLE_SETTINGS=Surface
screen.WATER_SHAPE_SETTINGS=Surface shape
screen.WATER_OPTICS_SETTINGS=Reflections and refraction
screen.WATER_COLOUR_SETTINGS=Colour and absorption
screen.LIGHTING_SKY_SETTINGS=Sky light
screen.LIGHTING_BLOCK_SETTINGS=Block light and bleed
screen.SHADOWS=Shadows
screen.SHADOW_SETTINGS=Real-time shadows
screen.SSS_SETTINGS=Screen-space shadows
screen.AO_SETTINGS=Ambient occlusion
screen.DEBUG_VIEWS=Debug views
screen.DEV_TOOLS=Developer switches
option.TAA=Temporal anti-aliasing
option.CLOUD_DEBUG=Cloud lighting diagnostic [0 off | 1 direct | 2 transmittance | 3 phase]
option.CLOUD_PHASE_FLAT=Cloud direct light (flat phase value)
option.CLOUD_TRANSMITTANCE_FLAT=Cloud direct light (flat transmittance)
option.CLOUD_LAYER_FALLOFF_BASE=Light at the cloud layer's bottom
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_NONE=Off
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_HEIGHT=Height channel
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_SMOOTHNESS=Smoothness
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_F0=Reflectance (F0)
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_NORMAL=Normal
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_MIP=Material detail limit
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_TANGENT_NORMAL=Tangent-space normal
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_MATERIAL_AO=Occlusion channel
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_SUBSURFACE=Porosity / scattering channel
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_EMISSION=Emission
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_REFLECTION=Reflection alone (4x) (diagnostic)
option.PBR_DEBUG.comment=Draws the resource pack's material data instead of \
	the picture, which is the quickest way to see what it actually provides. Each \
	material channel gets a view of its own: flat grey or flat white means the \
	pack supplies nothing for that block, and black in Tangent-space normal means \
	a texel from outside the sprite.

option.GLASS_REFLECTIONS=Glass reflections
option.GLASS_REFLECTIONS.comment=Whether glass and stained glass reflect the \
	way water and ice do. Glass reflects about 4% of the light head on and nearly \
	all of it at a glancing angle, so a window is mostly seen through until it \
	turns into a mirror from the side; without this a pane has no specular \
	response. They share the water and ice code and fade out with the sky light, \
	leaving glass underground and deep indoors unreflective. Water and ice always \
	reflected.

option.MOTION_BLUR=Motion blur
option.MOTION_BLUR.comment=Smears the image along the camera's travel through \
	the world, so walking, running, falling, riding a boat and being knocked back \
	all leave a trail. Working per pixel, walking sends the ground rushing past \
	while the horizon barely moves. Travel and turning blur, but the walk bob \
	does not, since a turn is only blurred past a few pixels a frame. Only the \
	camera is blurred: a mob, a river or a falling block stays sharp, and the \
	hand is left out entirely.

option.MOTION_BLUR_AMOUNT=Blur amount
option.MOTION_BLUR_AMOUNT.comment=How long the blur is, as a multiple of the \
	pixel's real movement: 1.0 is honest, lower hints at movement, higher \
	exaggerates it. No effect while Motion blur is off.

option.MOTION_BLUR_SAMPLES=Samples
option.MOTION_BLUR_SAMPLES.comment=How many samples are taken along the \
	movement, and so how smooth the blur is. Each one is a full-screen texture \
	fetch on every pixel, which is what the effect costs. No effect while Motion \
	blur is off.

option.TAA.comment=Averages the per-frame noise away over time. This is not \
	edge anti-aliasing - SMAA does that, and better. It solves the screen-space \
	shadows, the ambient occlusion and the godrays scattering their samples \
	differently every frame at a given pixel: averaging one surface point over \
	time is the only thing that removes that noise. On by default, on the \
	anti-aliasing page.

option.TAA_STRENGTH=Anti-aliasing strength
option.TAA_STRENGTH.comment=How much of the accumulated history each frame \
	keeps, for a pixel that did not move. It decides how much noise averages \
	away, the dither of the distant screen-space shadows and of the sky; a longer \
	history leaves less of both. A moved pixel keeps seven tenths of this, so \
	raising it does not ghost behind moving objects.

option.TAA_JITTER=Jitter radius
option.TAA_JITTER.comment=The radius of the sub-pixel offset, in pixels. 1 \
	puts each frame's sample anywhere in the pixel, which is what anti-aliasing \
	wants; 0 turns the offset off, leaving nothing to average.

option.TAA_CLAMP=History clamp
option.TAA_CLAMP.comment=How far outside the current frame's neighbourhood the \
	history may fall, in standard deviations. It stops moving objects dragging \
	their old positions behind them and textures being averaged into a smudge; \
	raise it if the image flickers, lower it if surfaces look smeared.


option.TAA_SHARPEN=Sharpening
option.TAA_SHARPEN.comment=How much to sharpen the resolved image, to counter \
	the softening from averaging frames. A little goes a long way; too much \
	brings back the aliasing it exists to remove.

option.SMAA=SMAA
option.SMAA.comment=Morphological anti-aliasing: it finds the edges already in \
	the finished image, works out the shape each makes, and blends across it by \
	the amount that shape calls for. It and Temporal anti-aliasing are \
	alternatives, so set Temporal anti-aliasing to Off and use this. It does not \
	denoise: the dither of the screen-space shadows, the ambient occlusion and \
	the godrays stays as drawn.

option.SMAA_THRESHOLD=SMAA Contrast Threshold
option.SMAA_THRESHOLD.comment=How far apart two neighbouring pixels have to be \
	before the edge between them is worth smoothing: lower finds more edges, \
	including a texture's own detail, which reads as blur rather than smoothing; \
	higher misses real edges.

option.SMAA_SEARCH_DISTANCE=SMAA Search Distance
option.SMAA_SEARCH_DISTANCE.comment=How far along an edge to look for where it \
	ends. An edge that continues past this does not end near the pixel, so \
	nothing is blended; lower is cheaper, and long diagonals stay less smooth.

option.SMAA_DEBUG=SMAA Status View
option.SMAA_DEBUG.comment=Replaces the frame with SMAA's own buffers: red is \
	the edges the first stage found, green the blend weight the second stage got \
	from the lookup table. Yellow lines on black means both stages ran and SMAA \
	works; red with no green means the lookup images are not reaching the second \
	stage; the ordinary picture means SMAA is off.

option.BLOOM=Bloom
option.BLOOM.comment=Whether the parts of the frame brighter than white spill \
	a glow onto what is around them. It happens before the tonemap, and that is \
	the part that matters: brighter than white only means anything before the \
	curve. It carries nothing across frames, so it cannot smear or accumulate. If \
	the picture looks hazy and flat, lower this or raise the threshold rather \
	than reaching for the tonemapping.
option.BLOOM_STRENGTH=Bloom strength
option.BLOOM_STRENGTH.comment=How much of the blurred light is added back - \
	whether the bright things glow or the lens looks smeared with vaseline. It \
	scales both the tight and the wide level, and is the only strength control.
option.BLOOM_THRESHOLD=Bloom threshold
option.BLOOM_THRESHOLD.comment=How bright a pixel has to be before any of it \
	blooms. The frame is linear light with 1.0 at white, so below that a pixel \
	has not brightened enough to spill. Lowering pulls ordinary lit surfaces into \
	the glow, thickening the effect but washing the contrast out; raising picks \
	out only what is genuinely overexposed - the sun, lava, a torch flame.
option.BLOOM_RADIUS=Bloom radius
option.BLOOM_RADIUS.comment=How far the glow spreads, as a multiple of the \
	blur's own width. Raising it widens the halo and costs nothing extra: the \
	passes pay for the tap count, not the radius.

option.BLOOM_EXCLUDE_SPECULAR=Bloom leaves out highlights
option.BLOOM_EXCLUDE_SPECULAR.comment=Whether the specular highlight on a \
	surface is taken back out of the glow. On by default: a highlight is a \
	picture of a light and not a light, so blooming it makes a shiny surface look \
	like a lamp of its own, and the cost is one more fetch per tap. Water, ice \
	and glass take their reflection from TranslucentLighting, so a highlight on \
	those still blooms - it is the sun glinting off water, which a camera really \
	does bloom.

option.PBR_EMISSION=Emission
option.PBR_EMISSION.comment=Whether the emission stored in the specular map's \
	alpha channel is added to the surface color: emissive blocks then glow with \
	their own color regardless of the light around them.

option.PBR_EMISSION_ANY_BLOCK=Emission on any block
option.PBR_EMISSION_ANY_BLOCK.comment=Whether the emission in a specular map \
	applies to any block or only to blocks the game itself treats as light \
	sources. On by default, and this is the LabPBR rule: the alpha channel is the \
	pack saying this part of my texture emits, which is how an ore vein or a \
	lamp's glass glows - and an ore is not a light source. Off is the older \
	behaviour, kept for packs that paint emission where they did not mean to or \
	leave the channel at maximum by accident: turn it off if something glows that \
	should not.

option.PBR_EMISSION_STRENGTH=Emission strength
option.PBR_EMISSION_STRENGTH.comment=How bright the emission from the resource \
	pack is. LabPBR stores it as a fraction of the surface color, rarely bright \
	enough alone to look lit from within because the tonemapper compresses it \
	back down.

option.BLOCK_EMISSION=Block self-emission
option.BLOCK_EMISSION.comment=Whether light sources glow with their own color: \
	glowstone, sea lanterns, lava, torches and so on. Unlike the emission above, \
	it needs nothing from the pack - it uses the marker Iris and OptiFine put in \
	the lightmap for faces that can emit. It lights the block itself, not its \
	surroundings.

option.BLOCK_EMISSION_STRENGTH=Self-emission strength
option.BLOCK_EMISSION_STRENGTH.comment=How bright block self-emission is. \
	Values above 1.0 push the surface past the white point of the tonemapper, \
	which is what a light source should look like.

option.BLOCKLIGHT_STRENGTH=Block light strength
option.BLOCKLIGHT_STRENGTH.comment=How bright block light is at full light \
	level. Its colour comes from the lightmap; raising this brightens everything \
	a torch lights without changing that colour.

option.BLOCKLIGHT_TEMPERATURE=Block light temperature
option.BLOCKLIGHT_TEMPERATURE.comment=The colour temperature of block light in \
	kelvin: it moves the lightmap's colour warmer or cooler. The scale is a \
	photographer's - a torch at 1800-2500 K reads deep orange, 2700 K the warm \
	white most people mean, daylight 5500-6500 K, and 7000 K and up blue. The \
	default 6500 K changes nothing; it changes hue only, not brightness.

option.LIGHT_TINT_STRENGTH=Light color tinting
option.LIGHT_TINT_STRENGTH.comment=How far a surface takes the colour of \
	nearby light sources rather than just having their light added on top. Adding \
	light alone cannot turn an orange-lit wall blue, so this also pulls the \
	surface's own colour towards the light around it; raise it for stronger \
	colour.

option.MIN_AMBIENT_BRIGHTNESS=Minimum ambient brightness
option.MIN_AMBIENT_BRIGHTNESS.comment=A floor under the ambient light, whether \
	or not anything lights a surface: without it, caves, the Nether and the End \
	sit near black. Values are linear light, so 0.1 is already several times the \
	pack's own floor - raise it until dark places are readable, 0.0 for the \
	original look.

option.COLORED_LIGHTS=Colored lights
option.COLORED_LIGHTS.comment=Whether light sources take the colour of the \
	light they give off - a soul lantern's blue, a redstone torch's red - instead \
	of one warm orange for all block light. The glowing block takes its own \
	light's colour and a screen-space bleed colours the surfaces around it; off \
	removes both and is the cheapest setting.

option.COLORED_LIGHTS_STRENGTH=Light source color
option.COLORED_LIGHTS_STRENGTH.comment=How far a light source's own surface \
	takes the colour of its light rather than its texture. At 1.0 it is lit \
	purely by the light's colour, and the texture's brightness pattern is kept \
	either way; lower it to keep glowing texture detail.

option.LIGHT_BLEED_STRENGTH=Light bleed strength
option.LIGHT_BLEED_STRENGTH.comment=How much light the sources on screen \
	spread onto surfaces around them - what tints the wall beside a soul lantern \
	blue. It adds light rather than recolouring: raise it until the colour reads, \
	lower it if nearby surfaces look washed out.

option.LIGHT_BLEED_RADIUS=Light bleed radius
option.LIGHT_BLEED_RADIUS.comment=How far the colour spreads, in pixels at \
	1080p scaled to your resolution. Costs nothing extra, but large values spread \
	a source's colour over most of the screen, where it stops reading as light.

option.LIGHT_BLEED_DEPTH=Light bleed depth tolerance
option.LIGHT_BLEED_DEPTH.comment=How far apart in depth two pixels may be \
	before light stops spreading between them; without it a source bleeds through \
	walls. Raise it if the colour cuts off too abruptly at edges, lower it if \
	light shines through a wall.

option.INDIRECT_BOUNCE=Indirect bounce
option.INDIRECT_BOUNCE.comment=Whether the light that bounces from one surface \
	onto another is drawn. A lit surface is a source too: a sunlit red wall puts \
	red on the white wall beside it. Being an approximation, it only blurs light \
	already on screen. Off by default, and worth a look rather than paying for.
option.INDIRECT_BOUNCE_STRENGTH=Indirect bounce strength
option.INDIRECT_BOUNCE_STRENGTH.comment=How much of the bounced light is \
	added. It takes light from what is already in the picture, so it can leave \
	the frame brighter and hazier - if the result looks washed out rather than \
	lit, lower it first.
option.INDIRECT_BOUNCE_FLOOR=Indirect bounce floor
option.INDIRECT_BOUNCE_FLOOR.comment=How bright a surface has to be to count \
	as giving light back, as a fraction of white. Ambient light is already in the \
	frame, so counting it all would make every surface bounce; raise this so only \
	genuinely bright things - sunlight, lava, a lit lamp - bounce.

option.END_SKY=End sky
value.END_SKY.END_SKY_AUTO=End only
value.END_SKY.END_SKY_ALWAYS=Everywhere
value.END_SKY.END_SKY_OFF=Off
option.END_SKY.comment=Whether the End gets a sky of its own: open space with \
	a starfield and a body in it, rather than the Overworld's atmosphere model \
	with its sun and horizon. End only picks it by dimension, which is what you \
	want; Everywhere is a test that draws the same sky in the Overworld and the \
	Nether too. The End has no sky light, so its islands are lit by Minimum \
	ambient brightness plus End light on this page; raise End light if the End is \
	too dark to navigate.

option.END_STAR_DENSITY=Star density
option.END_STAR_DENSITY.comment=How crowded the End's starfield is: 0.0 is an \
	empty sky, and each step above the default roughly doubles the stars drawn.

option.END_STAR_BRIGHTNESS=Star brightness
option.END_STAR_BRIGHTNESS.comment=How bright the stars are. They are the only \
	thing in the End brighter than a lit block, so this sets how far the sky \
	dominates the view.

option.END_STAR_SIZE=End star size
option.END_STAR_SIZE.comment=How large a single star is drawn, as a multiple \
	of its natural size. Each star is a disc in its own cell of the starfield \
	grid, so this scales that disc's radius: how big the stars look, not how many \
	there are, which Star density decides. The steps stop at 2.5 because the \
	starfield only looks at the cell the view direction falls into, so a disc \
	past about half a cell would be clipped square.

option.END_STAR_TINT=Star tint
option.END_STAR_TINT.comment=How far the End's starfield is moved towards \
	violet. 0.0 leaves it exactly as it was, hot blue through to cool orange; 1.0 \
	is a violet field. It does not paint every star the same violet - both ends \
	of the range move together, so the field keeps its warm and cool variation. \
	Only the End's field is affected; the Overworld's night stars are a different \
	implementation. 1.0 by default as of v0.7.

option.END_GIANT=End body
option.END_GIANT.comment=What sits in the End's sky: a very small white-hot \
	centre, a bright ring bent out of the sky behind it - the Einstein ring - a \
	wide faint halo, and the starfield and nebula it bends. The End has no sky \
	light, so its islands are lit only by the pack's direct light, which there \
	comes from a single direction, and the body is drawn where that light comes \
	from. This is the switch for the whole page; with it off the End is the \
	turning sky and nothing else.

option.END_GIANT_SIZE=Body size
option.END_GIANT_SIZE.comment=The angular radius of the body, in radians, and \
	the scale everything else is measured against; the real sun is 0.0045, so \
	every step is many times life size. With no visible disc left, it scales the \
	core's width, the halo's radius and the Einstein radius. Default about 15 \
	degrees across, largest about 26; raising it costs a little.

option.END_GIANT_GLOW=Body brightness
option.END_GIANT_GLOW.comment=The overall multiplier, applied to everything at \
	the end. It leaves the layers' ratios alone, so it is the one to use when the \
	whole body should be brighter or dimmer.

option.END_GIANT_CORE=Core overexposure
option.END_GIANT_CORE.comment=How bright the white-hot centre is, in linear \
	light. It is deliberately far above white - the brightest thing in the \
	dimension - and it is the only part of the body that carries its own light. \
	At 0.0 only the ring and the halo are left, and the centre goes completely \
	dark.

option.END_GIANT_LENS=Gravitational lensing
option.END_GIANT_LENS.comment=How hard the body bends the sky behind it. It \
	drags that sky radially around the body, stretching stars near the ring into \
	arcs, and piles the mapping up into a bright ring - the Einstein ring. If it \
	seems to do nothing, check Nebula band first: a black sky with a few points \
	in it, bent, is still a black sky with a few moved points. At 0.0 the sky is \
	not bent at all.

option.END_GIANT_LENS_RING=Einstein ring size
option.END_GIANT_LENS_RING.comment=The Einstein radius, as a multiple of the \
	body's radius: how far out the sky is dragged into a ring. This sets how \
	large the effect is, while Gravitational lensing sets how strong it is. \
	Default 2.2 puts the ring just outside the star; larger wraps more sky in, \
	smaller brings it in against the body.

option.END_GIANT_NEBULA=Nebula band
option.END_GIANT_NEBULA.comment=How strong the nebula band across the End's \
	sky is. It is not decoration: it is what makes the lensing visible, since \
	bending a nearly black sky with a few points in it gives back the same sky \
	with the points moved. One noise fetch, inside the band only. At 0.0 the sky \
	is plain black and Gravitational lensing has almost nothing to show.

option.END_GIANT_MOTION=Body motion
option.END_GIANT_MOTION.comment=Whether the End's sky turns. The starfield \
	turns with the nebula, since they are one sky and turning them apart would \
	slide one across the other. The turn is about the nebula band's own axis, the \
	one axis that leaves the band in place and lets only its contents stream. The \
	body itself does not turn - it is radially symmetric, so nothing would show. \
	With this off, the picture is completely still.

option.END_GIANT_MOTION_SPEED=Motion speed
option.END_GIANT_MOTION_SPEED.comment=The angular rate of the sky's turn, as a \
	multiplier. At 1.0 the sky comes round in about three minutes, a little under \
	two degrees a second - visible movement without being a carousel; the top \
	step, 5.0, is about 36 seconds a turn. Turn it down to 0.25 to be left alone, \
	or past 2.0 for an obvious stream of stars.

option.END_GIANT_HALO=Body halo
option.END_GIANT_HALO.comment=The wide, very faint glow around the body, \
	deliberately inconspicuous: you should not notice it, but with it off the sky \
	around the body looks hard. It stops the body looking pasted on. 0.10 by \
	default; raising it a long way colours the whole End.

option.END_GIANT_TINT=Body colour
option.END_GIANT_TINT.comment=The body's colour, from a warm white at 0.0 to \
	the End's own violet at 1.0: 0.0 neutral warm white, 0.65 a clear lavender, \
	1.0 - the default as of v0.7 - fully violet. The End's volumetric fog follows \
	it too: the End has no sun, so its air takes the body's colour rather than \
	the direct light's, and the two cannot disagree. The End's underwater fog is \
	deliberately not affected, since the water column has its own absorption \
	colour.

option.END_AMBIENT=End light
option.END_AMBIENT.comment=How much light the End has of its own. Every other \
	dimension is lit by its sky; the End has none, so without this its islands \
	are lit by nothing but Minimum ambient brightness, a dim but readable floor, \
	and this lays a violet light on top of it. It brightens rather than lights: \
	the End is not dark once Minimum ambient brightness is in place. Raise it to \
	see the End better; 0.35 is readable but plainly dusky, 1.5 something like \
	dusk. On by default at 1.0 as of v0.7.
option.END_DEBUG=End detection debug view
option.END_DEBUG.comment=Replaces the sky with what the checks for "is this \
	the End" returned: red is the dimension uniform, green the biome category, \
	blue the pack's own conclusion. In the End, white means all three agree and \
	black means none does - forcing End sky to Everywhere makes the effect run in \
	that case. It also tints what each program draws, so a patch of block texture \
	in the sky turning one of those colours names the program drawing it (green \
	eyes, blue glint, white beacon beam, black lines and outline, magenta weather \
	and entities, orange block entities, teal water and translucents, purple \
	translucent entities, yellow held item, grey breaking overlay). Diagnostic; \
	off by default.

option.FANCY_TRANSLUCENTS=Fancy translucents
option.FANCY_TRANSLUCENTS.comment=Gives water, ice and glass their own \
	shading: reflections, refraction and the absorption that darkens things with \
	depth. Off shades them like solid blocks - faster, but a window and a pool of \
	water both become flat sheets of colour.

option.VANILLA_ISH_WATER=Vanilla-style water
option.VANILLA_ISH_WATER.comment=Draws the water surface with the water \
	texture and vanilla's flat alpha instead of tinting it towards the sky and \
	fading it by reflection. Off, the default, is what makes water reflective \
	where the sky reaches it and opaque where it does not.

option.SCREENSPACE_REFLECTIONS=Screen-space reflections
option.SCREENSPACE_REFLECTIONS.comment=Whether water, ice and glass reflect \
	the world and not only the sky, by tracing the reflected ray through the \
	depth buffer. Only what is already on screen can be reflected, so the edges \
	of the view and anything hidden behind the surface fall back to the sky.

option.SCREENSPACE_REFRACTION=Screen-space refraction
option.SCREENSPACE_REFRACTION.comment=Whether what is seen through a water \
	surface is displaced by its waves, by how deep the water is behind it. \
	Deliberately not physically correct: it only moves the background a little, \
	enough to read as water without making it hard to see through.

option.WATER_PARALLAX=Water parallax
option.WATER_PARALLAX.comment=Offsets the water surface along the view ray by \
	the height of its waves, so ripples have depth at a glancing angle instead of \
	looking painted onto a flat sheet. Noise water only, as it is the surface \
	with waves to displace by.

option.WATER_PARALLAX_DISTANCE=Water parallax distance
option.WATER_PARALLAX_DISTANCE.comment=Distance in metres at which water \
	parallax fades out. Beyond it a pixel covers more than a wave, so the effect \
	describes nothing and only costs samples.

option.WATER_AA=Water antialiasing
option.WATER_AA.comment=How much the wave normals are smoothed, which stops \
	distant water shimmering where the fine waves fall below a pixel. Smoothing \
	also flattens the water: Off keeps every wave and its aliasing, High is the \
	calmest and least detailed.

option.NUM_CRESTS=Wave crests
option.NUM_CRESTS.comment=How many large, slow crest waves the water surface \
	is built from. Noise water only; more crests make a choppier sea at a higher \
	cost.

option.NUM_RIPPLES=Wave ripples
option.NUM_RIPPLES.comment=How many fine, fast ripple waves are added on top \
	of the crests. Noise water only.

option.NUM_CAUSTICS=Caustic waves
option.NUM_CAUSTICS.comment=How many waves the underwater caustic pattern is \
	built from. Each adds a set of bright lines, so lower values are cheaper and \
	blobbier.

option.WATER_SURFACE=Water surface
option.WATER_SURFACE.comment=Which model the water surface uses. Noise \
	generates waves procedurally and every other water option builds on it; Basic \
	is flat with no waves - the cheapest, and the one to pick if water is causing \
	trouble.

option.WATER_CAUSTICS=Water caustics
option.WATER_CAUSTICS.comment=The moving pattern of light that sunlit water \
	casts onto the terrain below it, brightened where the light is focused. It \
	comes from the shadow map, so it reaches only as far as the shadow distance \
	and fades out there.
option.WATER_CAUSTICS_DISTANCE=Water caustics distance

to whatever comes through it.\n\n\
Absorption on its own can only take colour away, so it gives water its deep \
blue-green but cannot make a lit body of water glow. This is the other half. \
Zero leaves the water exactly as it was, which is why that is the default: \
raising it is a change to the water's colour, so it is a choice.
