value.WATER_CHARACTER.TROPICAL=Tropical

value.WATER_ABSORPTION_METHOD.NO_ABSORPTION=No absorption
value.WATER_ABSORPTION_METHOD.REFRACTION_ASSISTED=Refraction assisted

option.MINISHITA_LIGHTING=Physical lighting

option.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING=Day sky lighting
option.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.comment=Daytime direct and ambient sky lighting: \
	Semi-natural keeps the sun near white with natural shadows, Fantasy warms and \
	saturates it, Retro flattens it towards an older shader.

option.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING=Night sky lighting
option.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.comment=Night-time sky lighting, on the same presets \
	as the daytime option: the moon replaces the sun, so it decides how blue and \
	bright a moonlit night is.

value.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.FANTASY=Fantasy
value.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.SEMI_NATURAL=Semi-natural
value.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.RETRO=Retro

value.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.FANTASY=Fantasy
value.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.SEMI_NATURAL=Semi-natural
value.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.RETRO=Retro

option.HELD_BLOCK_LIGHTING=Held light
option.HELD_BLOCK_LIGHTING.comment=Whether the block or item in your hand \
	lights the world around you like a lamp. It is on top of the light Minecraft \
	gives that block, so a torch in hand lights the ground at your feet.

option.SOFTER_SHADOWS=Softer shadows
option.SOFTER_SHADOWS.comment=Whether the shadow map is sampled four times \
	instead of once, each a hardware-filtered 2x2 tap - sixteen texels in all. It \
	keeps shadow edges from looking like a staircase, at three extra taps.

option.SHADOW_SOFTNESS=Shadow softness
option.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.comment=How far apart the four shadow taps are placed: \
	higher is softer and blurrier. At the low end the taps overlap and the extra \
	samples buy nothing.

option.SHADOW_DISTORT_FACTOR=Shadow distortion factor
option.SHADOW_DISTORT_FACTOR.comment=How far the shadow sampling position is \
	pushed along the surface normal, on faces pointing away from the light. This \
	is the fixed part of the shadow bias: lower it for closer, darker contact \
	shadows, raise it if surfaces show shadows they should not be casting on \
	themselves.

option.shadowMapResolution=Shadow map resolution
option.shadowMapResolution.comment=The size of the shadow map, deciding how \
	much detail a shadow can resolve. Higher is sharper and costs memory and fill \
	rate; blocky shadow edges are fixed here, since softness only blurs what \
	resolution already resolved.

option.REAL_TIME_SHADOWS=Real-time shadows
option.REAL_TIME_SHADOWS.comment=Whether terrain, entities and block entities \
	cast shadows through the shadow map. Off leaves only static light-map \
	shadows: much cheaper, but nothing moving casts anything and you cannot stand \
	behind the sun.

option.REAL_TIME_ENTITY_SHADOWS=Real-time entity shadows
option.REAL_TIME_BLOCK_ENTITY_SHADOWS=Real-time block entity shadows

option.COLORED_SHADOWS=Coloured shadows
option.COLORED_SHADOWS.comment=Whether light through stained glass arrives \
	with the glass's colour, so a red pane lays a red patch of sunlight on the \
	floor rather than a hole in it. Stained glass does not block light, so it \
	casts a colour and nothing else: the patch under a window is as bright as the \
	ground around it. The colour comes from the glass's texture and applies to \
	sunlight and moonlight alone.

option.COLORED_SHADOWS_STRENGTH=Coloured shadow strength
option.COLORED_SHADOWS_STRENGTH.comment=How much colour the light takes on, \
	from 0.0 to 1.0, for stained glass and the nether portal. At 1.0 the patch \
	under a window is the glass's colour, so black glass puts the floor in the \
	dark; hence a setting rather than a constant; towards 0.0 keeps the light, \
	taking only the hue.

option.COLORED_SHADOWS_PORTAL=Nether portal glow
option.COLORED_SHADOWS_PORTAL.comment=Whether a nether portal tints the light \
	around it, so the ground in front of one is lit violet rather than by plain \
	sunlight. The violet keeps its brightest channel at full strength, so it \
	never dims the ground - it only takes the green out of the sunlight - and it \
	is not drawn on translucent surfaces, so a portal does not colour its own \
	faces.

option.DIRECTIONAL_SKYLIGHT_SHADING=Directional sky light
option.DIRECTIONAL_SKYLIGHT_SHADING.comment=Whether sky light depends on which \
	way a surface faces, making upward faces brighter. Off gives every face the \
	same sky light: flatter, and better if undersides look too dark.

option.GODRAYS=Volumetric fog and shafts
option.GODRAYS.comment=Whether light shafts radiate from the sun, drawn from a \
	mask of where the sky is visible. Being a screen-space effect, it only knows \
	what is on screen: something off the edge of the view cannot block a shaft. \
	Under water the mask is empty, since the surface hides the sky from below; \
	Underwater light shafts puts them back.

option.GODRAYS_STRENGTH=Volumetric light strength
option.GODRAYS_STRENGTH.comment=How bright the light shafts are, as a multiple \
	of what the pack draws on its own. It covers both sides of the waterline: the \
	underwater shafts have a strength of their own and the two multiply, so under \
	water both apply and above it only this one. Setting either to 0.0 turns off \
	the part it covers, the same picture as with Volumetric light off. 1.0 is the \
	pack's own picture, unchanged.

option.RAIN_COLOR_SATURATION=Rain colour saturation
option.RAIN_COLOR_SATURATION.comment=How much colour the particle itself \
	carries. 1.0 is the texture's own pale blue-grey, 0.0 pure grey, and higher \
	pushes what colour there is. Colour only, not opacity - for heavier or \
	lighter rain use Rain drop size. Rain only - a snowflake is left as the resource pack draws it.

option.RAIN_DROP_AMOUNT=Rain drop amount
option.RAIN_DROP_AMOUNT.comment=How many times the rain texture is tiled \
	across each rain column. Higher packs more drops into the same space and \
	reads as denser rain, lower spreads them out. This sets how many drops there \
	are, not how thick they are - that is Rain drop size. 1.0 is vanilla, rain only: \
	a snowflake is left as the resource pack draws it.

option.RAIN_DROP_SIZE=Rain drop size
option.RAIN_DROP_SIZE.comment=How much of each drop's original width is kept. \
	Lower makes the streaks thinner, 1.0 leaves the particle as it was. 0.1-0.2 \
	is a drizzle, below that the rain nearly vanishes. Rain only - a snowflake is left as the resource pack draws it.

option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_DENSITY=Fog density
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_DENSITY.comment=How thick the air is, per block - the \
	master dial for the whole effect. Raise it and the low ground fills up, lower \
	it and the shafts thin out. It also sets how fast the shafts fade with \
	height.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_HEIGHT=Fog height
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_HEIGHT.comment=How many blocks up the fog thins to a \
	third of its density at the bottom: larger makes a deeper layer, smaller \
	keeps it on the ground.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_BASE=Fog base height
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_BASE.comment=The height the fog is measured from; 62 is \
	Minecraft's sea level, so the layer pools in valleys and along water and \
	thins on hills. Raising it lifts the layer, lowering it puts the thick air \
	below ground where only caves see it.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_DISTANCE=March distance
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_DISTANCE.comment=How far the ray may reach, in blocks; \
	past it the medium is not sampled. It leaves no hole in the distance, since \
	the pack's own per-fragment fog fades the far world out.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_STEPS=March steps
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_STEPS.comment=How many times the ray samples the medium \
	- the cost of the effect. 24 is where banding stops showing; below that, \
	rings appear around the light.

option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_NOISE=Fog structure
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_NOISE.comment=Whether the medium has a shape of its own. \
	0 is an even haze; raising it gathers the fog into patches with gaps between \
	them. With no structure only the shadow test varies, so every texel the \
	shadow map moves makes the fog jump.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_SCALE=Fine fog scale
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_SCALE.comment=How large the patches are, in blocks: \
	larger is softer and wider, smaller more broken up.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_OCTAVES=Fog noise layers
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_OCTAVES.comment=How many layers of noise are summed. \
	More layers is finer detail, and each costs two noise fetches per step.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_OCTAVE_SCALE=Fog layer scale
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_OCTAVE_SCALE.comment=How much smaller each layer is than \
	the one before it.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_OCTAVE_FADE=Fog layer fade
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_OCTAVE_FADE.comment=How much weaker each layer is than \
	the one before it.

option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_TIME_VARIATION=Fog follows the time of day
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_TIME_VARIATION.comment=Whether the medium's density \
	follows the time of day. 0 is the same all day; 1 follows the day: thickest \
	at sunrise and sunset, thinning through the morning until noon is nearly \
	bare, then building again towards the next sunrise. The curve's shape is \
	fixed.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_RAIN_STRENGTH=Thicker in rain
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_RAIN_STRENGTH.comment=How much thicker the fog is in \
	rain. 0 leaves it unchanged; 1 doubles it at full rain, and the weather term \
	is squared, so a drizzle barely registers while a storm closes the view in. \
	It drives both the marched medium and the pack's own distance fog, and the \
	second is what shows. Only applies out in the open: rain does not thicken the \
	air in a cave.
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_FULL_RES=Full resolution fog
option.VOLUMETRIC_FOG_FULL_RES.comment=Whether to march and store the fog at \
	the full resolution of the frame. Off by default at a quarter of the frame, \
	which the default step count is tuned for. Warning: full resolution costs \
	about sixteen times as much. Turn it on only to see what the upscale was \
	hiding. Compile-time, so it reloads the shaders.

option.NETHER_PLUMES=Nether plumes
option.NETHER_PLUMES.comment=Draws columns of smoke rising through the \
	Nether's air, plus a layer of it under the ceiling. This can only add light, \
	never take it away: the plumes glow rather than blot out what is behind them. \
	It runs over the finished frame, so it cannot leave a seam on Distant \
	Horizons or Voxy terrain. It depends on Volumetric fog and shafts - if the \
	plumes do not appear, check that first. If they look sparse or grainy, \
	raising the march distance (96 to 128 or 192) does more than raising the \
	density.

option.NETHER_PLUME_DENSITY=Plume density
option.NETHER_PLUME_DENSITY.comment=How much smoke there is. 0.0 is no plumes \
	at all; the layer under the ceiling has a switch of its own. Warning: too \
	high and the Nether becomes a lamp rather than a place.

option.NETHER_CEILING_SMOKE=Ceiling smoke
option.NETHER_CEILING_SMOKE.comment=The smoke that gathers under the ceiling. \
	Not the same thing as the plumes: a flat layer with no noise in it, \
	thickening as the third power of the height above y=40. It is what gives the \
	Nether a roof. 0.0 is no ceiling smoke.

option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS=Underwater light shafts
option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS.comment=Whether the light shafts are drawn when the \
	camera is in water as well as in air. Under water the mask is empty, because \
	the surface hides the sky completely, so the shafts vanish; this puts them \
	back by asking the same question of the water, where the light comes through \
	the surface and the terrain gets in the way. They run along the sun's own \
	direction, so they meet the sun actually drawn in the sky. Needs Volumetric \
	light on, and nothing is drawn in lava or powder snow.

option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS_STRENGTH=Underwater light shaft strength
option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS_STRENGTH.comment=How bright the shafts under water \
	are, relative to the ones above the waterline at the same time of day. It \
	multiplies with Volumetric light strength - under water both apply, above it \
	only that one - and setting either to 0.0 turns off the part it covers. At \
	1.0 water and air are equally bright.

option.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS=Enchant glint brightness
option.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.comment=How bright the moving sparkle on an enchanted \
	item is; this is the glint layer rather than the item itself. One program \
	draws all three places at once - the held item in first and third person, and \
	the armour worn - so one cannot be changed alone, and an item with no \
	enchantment is not affected. 1.0 is the glint exactly as the pack draws it \
	without this option. It is stronger than it looks: the game blends with \
	SRC_COLOR, so the light added grows with the square of this number, and 2.0 \
	adds four times as much. Step down if the item's surface washes out around \
	the streaks, step up if it cannot be seen in daylight.

value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.1.0=Off (vanilla)
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.1.5=Subtle
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.2.0=Bright (default)
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.3.0=Strong
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.4.0=Very strong
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.6.0=Extreme

option.NIGHT_DESATURATION_EFFECT=Night desaturation
option.NIGHT_DESATURATION_EFFECT.comment=Drains colour out of a surface as the \
	light around it goes away, roughly as an eye does in the dark. Applied per \
	surface from its own sky and block light rather than to the whole screen, so \
	a torch in a dark room stays coloured.

option.TONEMAP=Tonemap
option.SKY_DITHER=Sky colour dithering (removes banding)

option.SATURATED_TONEMAP=Saturated tonemap
option.SATURATED_TONEMAP.comment=A tonemapping curve that keeps colour as \
	things brighten instead of washing them towards white, so sunsets, stained \
	glass and light sources stay colourful at the cost of some detail in the \
	brightest highlights.

option.ATMOSPHERE_MODEL=Atmosphere model

option.DAY_ATMOSPHERE=Day atmosphere
option.DAY_ATMOSPHERE.comment=The day's sky and sunlight preset: sky gradient, \
	sun colour and how fast the atmosphere thickens with distance - the biggest \
	influence on the look here.

option.NIGHT_ATMOSPHERE=Night atmosphere
option.NIGHT_ATMOSPHERE.comment=The night's sky and moonlight preset, set \
	separately from the day's.

option.HARDER_HORIZON_DAY=Harder horizon (day)
option.HARDER_HORIZON_DAY.comment=Sharpens the sky's soft horizon gradient \
	into a band of colour. Made for the Retro atmospheres; with the others it \
	looks wrong.

option.HARDER_HORIZON_NIGHT=Harder horizon (night)
option.HARDER_HORIZON_NIGHT.comment=The same for the night sky, and set \
	separately from the day one.

option.BORDER_FOG=Border fog
option.BORDER_FOG.comment=Fades the far edge of the render distance into the \
	sky colour, so the world does not stop at a hard line, and covers the gap \
	Distant Horizons terrain would leave.

option.ATMOSPHERE_FOG_STRENGTH_NOON=Atmosphere thickness
option.ATMOSPHERE_FOG_STRENGTH_NOON.comment=How thick the atmosphere is at \
	noon: lower keeps distant terrain clear and contrasty for building, higher \
	makes a landscape recede.

option.PLANAR_CLOUD_LAYERS=Planar clouds
option.PLANAR_CLOUD_LAYERS.comment=Draws the clouds as flat layers of a \
	procedural texture instead of Minecraft's own boxes: thicker and softer, lit \
	by the sun and from underneath at sunset.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUDS=Blocky volumetric clouds
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUDS.comment=Draws the pack's own cloud layer - a deck of \
	cube-shaped cells twelve blocks on a side - instead of Minecraft's flat cloud \
	boxes, which are off while it is on. Terrain hides the clouds behind it \
	rather than the clouds drawing over it. A cell is a real box, so the layer \
	has a top and a bottom, a darker underside, and a bright rim where a cloud \
	stands in front of the sun. The cost is one noise fetch per cell the view ray \
	crosses, on every sky pixel - the most expensive thing in the pack's sky, so \
	lower the step count first. It casts no shadow and costs nothing on the \
	ground.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_CELL_SIZE=Cloud size
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_CELL_SIZE.comment=How big one cloud is, in blocks: the \
	cell size and the size the noise is stretched to. Smaller breaks the layer \
	into many small cubes with more sky between them, larger gives fewer, bigger \
	clouds and is cheaper, since the ray crosses fewer cells.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_COVERAGE=Cloud coverage
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_COVERAGE.comment=How much of the sky the layer covers, \
	from nearly empty to overcast; 0.5 is the tuned value. Weather thickens it on \
	its own whatever this is set to, and slowly - over about a minute, since the \
	clouds follow the game's wetness rather than the rain itself.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_HEIGHT=Cloud height
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_HEIGHT.comment=The height of the layer's underside, in \
	blocks. 192 is where Minecraft's own clouds sit in 1.18 and up, so vanilla \
	clouds land in the same place. Raising it sends the layer towards the \
	horizon, lowering it pushes it into the terrain, where on a mountain you can \
	end up inside it.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_THICKNESS=Cloud thickness
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_THICKNESS.comment=How deep the layer is, in blocks - \
	what a sight line travels through inside a cloud. It therefore scales how \
	opaque the clouds are: thin ones read as haze, thick ones as solid. The \
	lighting comes from the gap between top and bottom, so too thin a layer looks \
	flat.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_SPEED=Cloud speed
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_SPEED.comment=How fast the layer drifts, as a multiple \
	of the wind the planar clouds move with. 0.0 stops it, useful for studying \
	one shape.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_STEPS=Cloud march steps
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_STEPS.comment=How many cloud cells the view ray may \
	cross before the march gives up. Empty sky is walked a cell at a time, so \
	every sky pixel pays this whether or not there is a cloud, and it alone \
	limits how far a shallow ray sees towards the horizon. Lower it first if the \
	layer costs too much, raise it if distant clouds end abruptly.

option.DEBUG=Debug view
option.DEBUG.comment=Replaces the image with one of the buffers the pack works \
	in, which is how to find out what it thinks is happening when something looks \
	wrong. Not meant to be played with.

option.FREEZE_ANIMATION_TIMER=Freeze animation timer
option.FREEZE_ANIMATION_TIMER.comment=Stops the frame timer, so water waves, \
	foliage and clouds hold still and a single frame can be looked at closely. \
	Everything else keeps running: this freezes the animations, not the game.

option.PREFER_FDM_GRADIENT=Prefer gradient fog
option.PREFER_FDM_GRADIENT.comment=Works out how much fog is in front of a \
	water surface with a coarser and cheaper method. Leave it off unless the \
	accurate path is what is giving you trouble.

value.SURFACE_COLORS.WORLD_POSITION=World position
value.SURFACE_COLORS.WORLD_NORMAL=World normal

value.DAY_ATMOSPHERE.RETRO=Retro
value.DAY_ATMOSPHERE.FANTASY=Fantasy
value.DAY_ATMOSPHERE.SEMI_NATURAL=Semi-natural

value.NIGHT_ATMOSPHERE.FANTASY=Fantasy
value.NIGHT_ATMOSPHERE.SEMI_NATURAL=Semi-natural
value.NIGHT_ATMOSPHERE.RETRO=Retro

value.DEBUG.DEBUG_NONE=None
value.DEBUG.DEBUG_GODRAYS_NOISY=Noisy godrays
value.DEBUG.DEBUG_GODRAYS_SMOOTH=Smooth godrays
value.DEBUG.DEBUG_SKYLIGHT=Sky light
value.DEBUG.DEBUG_DEPTH=Depth buffer (is distant terrain in it)

option.SCREENSPACE_SHADOWS=Screen-space shadows (distance)
option.SCREENSPACE_SHADOWS.comment=Shadows for terrain the shadow map does not \
	reach: a ray is walked from the fragment towards the sun through the depth \
	buffer, and shadowed wherever something is in the way. It applies only past \
	the shadow distance. Two limits are inherent: the ray only reaches geometry \
	on screen, and thin things such as grass and fences are easy to miss. Noise \
	in the distance: raise the step count or shorten the ray. Overall grey: lower \
	the strength.

option.SSS_STRENGTH=Shadow strength
option.SSS_STRENGTH.comment=How much the distance shadows darken. It \
	multiplies the whole colour: sunlight and sky light are already added \
	together by then, so a value near 1.0 takes some sky light with it too.

option.SSS_DARK_LIMIT=Maximum darkening
option.SSS_DARK_LIMIT.comment=The most this effect may darken a pixel, as a \
	fraction of its own colour: 0.5 is no darker than half. It multiplies the \
	whole colour, already one number by this point, so strength and darkness were \
	one dial - at full strength a fully occluded pixel keeps a quarter of its \
	colour, which on a dim surface is a hole rather than a shadow.

option.SSS_STEPS=Ray steps
option.SSS_STEPS.comment=How many steps the ray takes. More steps means finer \
	ones over the same length, which keeps it from slipping between two pieces of \
	terrain and leaving streaks of light; each costs a depth sample.

option.SSS_LENGTH=Ray length
option.SSS_LENGTH.comment=How far the ray reaches, in blocks. A valley floor \
	often sits several dozen blocks below the ridge that shadows it, so keep this \
	generous.

option.CLOUD_MULTIPLE_SCATTER=Scattering inside the cloud
option.CLOUD_MULTIPLE_SCATTER_OCTAVES=Scattering passes
option.CLOUD_MULTIPLE_SCATTER_FALLOFF=Scattering falloff
option.VIGNETTE=Screen vignette
option.VIGNETTE_STRENGTH=Vignette strength
option.VIGNETTE_START=Vignette start
option.VIGNETTE_END=Vignette reaches full
option.WATER_REFLECTION_STRENGTH=Water reflection strength
option.WATER_REFLECTION_STRENGTH.comment=How much of the water's reflection to \
	keep. 1.0 keeps every bit of it; turning it down keeps the water's own colour \
	and Fresnel edge while taking the picture out, since a reflection is what \
	makes water read as a shader rather than as water. 0.0 is no reflection at \
	all, water lit and fogged like any other surface. It does not go above 1, \
	because a Fresnel term is the fraction of light reflected and cannot exceed \
	all of it, so a larger setting gives the same picture as 1.0. Water only; ice \
	and glass keep every bit of theirs.

option.WATER_SKY_BODIES=Sun and moon reflections

option.WATER_SUN_SIZE=Sun reflection trim
option.WATER_SUN_SIZE.comment=How much larger or smaller the sun is in the \
	water than in the sky. 1.000 is the same size; the range is 0.700 to 1.200 in \
	half-percent steps, and the default of 1.075 was measured against the sky \
	rather than meant to be copied. The fine control that lines up the reflected \
	body size above.
option.WATER_MOON_SIZE=Moon reflection trim
option.WATER_MOON_SIZE.comment=The same as the sun's size, for the moon alone, \
	because the sun nearly fills its own square while the moon is a disc painted \
	inside one - the two need very different factors to come out the same size. \
	The default of 0.740 is that fact measured.
option.WATER_BODY_SIZE=Sky body size
option.WATER_BODY_TILT=Celestial axis tilt
option.WATER_BODY_TILT.comment=Which axis the bodies turn about, which is the \
	plane of their orbit rather than a picture's orientation. 0 puts the axis \
	straight up, leaving them circling the horizon and barely rising; 90 lays it \
	flat and north-south, taking them straight over the top. The default of 48.4 \
	was measured against the sky. It is a direction rather than a rotation axis, \
	so values past ninety are meaningful and should not be normalised back into a \
	right angle: 131.6 and -48.4 are this value and its mirror.
option.WATER_BODY_SIZE.comment=How large the sun and the moon are in the \
	water, measured as the half-angle of the whole image: 0.96 is the size the \
	game draws them at, about 16.5 degrees. This is the coarse control, and the \
	two trims below line it up. Little over a quarter of the image is the lit \
	body, so raise this towards 1 for a smaller, tighter reflection.
option.WATER_BODY_BRIGHTNESS=Reflected body brightness
option.WATER_BODY_BRIGHTNESS.comment=How bright the sun and the moon are in \
	the water's reflection of the sky. The pack's sky model has no disc in it, so \
	the two are drawn into the reflection, water only, at this many times their \
	brightness in the sky. It also governs the End, where the dimension's own \
	body glares off its water. 1.0 is as bright as the sky with no glare at all; \
	the default of 32.0 is a clear reflection of the body on the water.
